Execute Phase D2 only.

GOAL
Retrieve real topic evidence before generating trend-aware ideas.

START WITH ONE SOURCE
Inspect current official YouTube Data API documentation.

Verify:
- Authentication.
- Available chart/search parameters.
- Quotas.
- Attribution.
- Caching and retention.
- Restrictions on derived metrics and storage.

Do not hard-code remembered quota figures.

Use current region/category popularity data and controlled
niche searches where appropriate.

Do not assume the popularity chart represents every niche
or every social platform.

NORMALIZED EVIDENCE
Store only permitted fields:
- Internal evidence ID.
- Provider.
- Source URL.
- Source/content identifier.
- Title or topic.
- Publication time where supplied.
- Retrieval time.
- Region/language.
- Available metrics.
- Metric observation time.
- Expiry/revalidation time.

Separate provider metrics from application-derived assessments.

GROWTH
A single observation cannot establish acceleration.
Only use a growth label when comparable permitted observations
support it.

If trend strength cannot be established:
Use “popular,” “recent,” or “niche inspiration” as appropriate.

SECURITY
Keep keys server-side.
Validate inputs.
Bound queries and pagination.
Sanitize external content.
Do not follow arbitrary user-supplied URLs.

CACHE
Share reusable public evidence appropriately across users,
but never mix private user preferences into public cache entries.

Respect provider retention rules.
Handle stale data, quota exhaustion, and partial failure.

CONFIGURATION
Add a server-side YouTube API key or supported credential method.
Request approval before provisioning external resources.

TEST
Perform a real niche query and a real region/category query.
Show sanitized normalized evidence and source URLs.

ACCEPTANCE
The app retrieves real, timestamped evidence and honestly
reports unavailable or insufficient data.

Stop after D2.
